{\pard\keepn\sb240\sa120\b\fs32 Methods\par}
{\pard\keepn\sb240\sa120\b\fs28 Anatomical preprocessing\par}
{\pard\keepn\sb240\sa120\b\fs24 Anatomical references\par}
{\pard\sa160\sl360\slmult1 For each participant, one T1-weighted (T1w) and one T2-weighted (T2w) reference were selected from all available sessions. The earliest acquisition of each modality was used. When no T1w image was available, a 1 mm T1w-like reference was synthesized from the T2w image with SynthSR (Iglesias et al., 2023).\par}
{\pard\keepn\sb240\sa120\b\fs24 Intensity and gradient correction\par}
{\pard\sa160\sl360\slmult1 Each source image was corrected for gradient nonlinearity with the Human Connectome Project (HCP) gradient-unwarping procedure (Glasser et al., 2013) when a site gradient profile matched the acquisition and its metadata did not report prior correction. A brain mask was estimated on each image with SynthStrip (Hoopes et al., 2022), and intensity nonuniformity was corrected within that mask with ANTs N4BiasFieldCorrection (Tustison et al., 2010).\par}
{\pard\keepn\sb240\sa120\b\fs24 Reference alignment\par}
{\pard\sa160\sl360\slmult1 The T1w reference was rigidly aligned to the pose of the MNI152NLin2009cAsym template (Fonov et al., 2011) with ANTs mutual-information registration (Avants et al., 2011). It was resampled once with cubic B-spline interpolation onto a template-oriented grid at the source resolution, and its brain mask was resampled with nearest-neighbor interpolation. When a T2w reference was available, it was aligned to the T1w reference with six-degree-of-freedom FSL FLIRT (Jenkinson & Smith, 2001).\par}
{\pard\keepn\sb240\sa120\b\fs24 Surface reconstruction\par}
{\pard\sa160\sl360\slmult1 Cortical surfaces were reconstructed from the T1w reference with FreeSurfer {\i recon-all} (Dale et al., 1999; Fischl, 2012). FreeSurfer skull stripping was replaced by the SynthStrip brain mask, resampled to FreeSurfer's conformed grid. Gray-matter, cortical-ribbon, and subcortical masks were derived from the FreeSurfer segmentation labels. White, pial, midthickness, and inflated surfaces and spherical registrations were exported to GIFTI with Connectome Workbench (Marcus et al., 2011). The fsaverage6 template geometry (40,962 vertices per hemisphere) was obtained from TemplateFlow (Ciric et al., 2022).\par}
{\pard\keepn\sb240\sa120\b\fs24 Spatial normalization\par}
{\pard\sa160\sl360\slmult1 The T1w reference was registered nonlinearly to the MNI152NLin2009cAsym template (Fonov et al., 2011) with ANTs (Avants et al., 2011). Rigid and affine stages used a mutual-information metric, and a final symmetric normalization (SyN) stage (Avants et al., 2008) used a cross-correlation metric. All stages used brain masks.\par}
{\pard\keepn\sb240\sa120\b\fs28 Functional preprocessing\par}
{\pard\keepn\sb240\sa120\b\fs24 Overview\par}
{\pard\sa160\sl360\slmult1 Each BOLD run was preprocessed once and published in the MNI152NLin2009cAsym, T1w, fsaverage6, and fsnative spaces. Slice-timing correction was not performed.\par}
{\pard\keepn\sb240\sa120\b\fs24 Simultaneous-slice artifact correction\par}
{\pard\sa160\sl360\slmult1 Signal shared among simultaneously acquired slices was estimated and removed in native scanner space with Multiband Artifact Regression in Simultaneous Slices (MARSS) (Tubiolo et al., 2024) for runs with a multiband acceleration factor of 6 or greater. The correction preceded all other processing.\par}
{\pard\keepn\sb240\sa120\b\fs24 Gradient correction and head motion\par}
{\pard\sa160\sl360\slmult1 BOLD, single-band reference (SBRef), and spin-echo images were corrected for gradient nonlinearity with the HCP gradient-unwarping procedure (Glasser et al., 2013) when a site gradient profile matched the acquisition and its metadata did not report prior correction. The BOLD displacement field was applied within the single final resampling. Head motion was estimated with FSL MCFLIRT (Jenkinson et al., 2002). A matched SBRef served as the registration reference when it passed metadata, pose, overlap, and similarity checks; otherwise a BOLD-derived reference was used.\par}
{\pard\keepn\sb240\sa120\b\fs24 Susceptibility distortion correction\par}
{\pard\sa160\sl360\slmult1 Susceptibility distortion was estimated with the first applicable method: FSL TOPUP (Andersson et al., 2003) with an opposite-phase-encoding spin-echo fieldmap pair; otherwise SynBOLD-DisCo (Yu et al., 2023), which synthesizes an undistorted BOLD reference from the T1w image for TOPUP; and otherwise nonlinear registration of the BOLD reference to the T1w anatomy with ANTs SyN (Avants et al., 2008).\par}
{\pard\keepn\sb240\sa120\b\fs24 Anatomical registration\par}
{\pard\sa160\sl360\slmult1 The distortion-corrected BOLD reference was aligned to the T1w anatomy with FreeSurfer boundary-based registration (Greve & Fischl, 2009), using the white surface, coreg initialization, and 6 degrees of freedom.\par}
{\pard\keepn\sb240\sa120\b\fs24 Resampling\par}
{\pard\sa160\sl360\slmult1 Per-frame motion, distortion, and anatomical transforms were composed and applied in a single interpolation with AFNI {\i 3dNwarpApply} (Cox, 1996), using wsinc5 interpolation for the signal and linear interpolation for the warps. Template-space outputs used the T1w-to-template transforms from anatomical preprocessing. Volumes were written on an anatomically oriented grid at the BOLD voxel size. Surface time series were sampled within the cortical ribbon between the white and pial surfaces with Connectome Workbench (Marcus et al., 2011).\par}
{\pard\keepn\sb240\sa120\b\fs24 Confounds and outlier frames\par}
{\pard\sa160\sl360\slmult1 Confound time series included the six rigid-body motion parameters; global, white-matter, and cerebrospinal-fluid signals; the temporal derivatives and quadratic terms of these signals (Satterthwaite et al., 2013); framewise displacement (FD) (Power et al., 2012) computed with a 50 mm head radius; 10 anatomical CompCor components from combined white-matter and cerebrospinal-fluid voxels (Behzadi et al., 2007); DVARS; and discrete cosine drift regressors with a 0.0078125 Hz (128 s) cutoff.\par}
{\pard\sa160\sl360\slmult1 Frames were marked as outliers when FD exceeded 1 mm or when DVARS met both the statistical criterion (one-sided test at a 5% Bonferroni-corrected family-wise error rate) and the practical criterion (change in percent D-var greater than 5%) of Afyouni and Nichols (2018). Up to 20 initial non-steady-state volumes were also marked.\par}
{\pard\keepn\sb240\sa120\b\fs28 Denoising\par}
{\pard\keepn\sb240\sa120\b\fs24 Nuisance regression and filtering\par}
{\pard\sa160\sl360\slmult1 Runs with fewer than 50 frames were excluded. When the smoothing FWHM was positive, data were smoothed with Connectome Workbench (Marcus et al., 2011) in the surface or volume geometry before temporal modeling, and volumetric smoothing was restricted to a gray-matter mask thresholded at 0.2.\par}
{\pard\sa160\sl360\slmult1 Frames marked by any outlier column were censored: they were excluded from model fitting, and their output values are model reconstructions. Nuisance regressors were global signal, FD, the six rigid-body motion parameters, the first five anatomical CompCor components, and the cosine drift terms. A real Fourier basis spanning 0.01 to 0.1 Hz was constructed, together with task regressors where events were available and detrending terms. On retained frames, nuisance regressors were projected into this passband, orthogonalized with respect to the task and detrending terms, standardized, and reduced by principal component analysis to the fewest components explaining 99% of their variance. At least 30 dimensions and 50% of the passband rank were left after nuisance removal, and nuisance regression was skipped when fewer than 30 dimensions remained before it. Each BOLD series was fitted to the remaining passband basis on retained frames and reconstructed at every frame, then standardized with the mean and standard deviation of retained frames.\par}
{\pard\keepn\sb240\sa120\b\fs28 Dynamic connectivity\par}
{\pard\keepn\sb240\sa120\b\fs24 Connectivity pooling\par}
{\pard\sa160\sl360\slmult1 Cleaned runs were pooled within each participant, space, and smoothing level. A run was included if cleaning was defined, at least 100 frames and 70% of its frames were retained, the cleaning model left at least 50 residual degrees of freedom, the participation effective rank of the cleaned data was at least 10, and no single temporal component explained more than 50% of its variance. Each participant required at least 1 usable run and 100 retained frames in total.\par}
{\pard\sa160\sl360\slmult1 Censored frames were omitted, and each admitted run was centered and standardized at every spatial location before pooling. Each run's contribution to the pooled covariance was weighted in proportion to the temporal degrees of freedom remaining after cleaning. The leading eigenspace of the weighted, pooled correlation matrix was estimated with a streamed randomized decomposition (Halko et al., 2011) of at most 1000 dimensions, with an oversampling of 16 and 1 power iteration, without forming the full location-by-location matrix. The fit was represented as a short synthetic series whose sample correlations reproduce the fitted low-rank correlation matrix, for seed-based connectivity in Connectome Workbench (Marcus et al., 2011).\par}
{\pard\keepn\sb240\sa120\b\fs28 Individualized networks\par}
{\pard\keepn\sb240\sa120\b\fs24 Individualized network estimation\par}
{\pard\sa160\sl360\slmult1 Each participant's pooled connectivity series formed a matrix with one row per spatial location and one column per fitted low-rank dimension. When it had more than 1000 columns, it was projected onto its leading 1000 randomized singular vectors (Halko et al., 2011) with an oversampling of 10 and 2 power iterations.\par}
{\pard\sa160\sl360\slmult1 Spatial independent component analysis with FastICA (Hyv\u228?rinen, 1999), as implemented in scikit-learn (Pedregosa et al., 2011), decomposed this matrix into 100 components, treating locations as observations, with unit-variance whitening, at most 500 iterations, and a tolerance of 0.0001. Each component was oriented so that its median loading was nonpositive. Its loadings were then clipped between zero and their 0.99 quantile across locations and divided by that quantile, giving a membership map bounded by zero and one. These maps are not calibrated probabilities and are not normalized across networks.\par}
{\pard\keepn\sb240\sa120\b\fs24 Network labeling\par}
{\pard\sa160\sl360\slmult1 Networks were given heuristic labels by spatial correspondence with population reference maps: the 15 networks of Du et al. (2024) and the probabilistic language atlas of Lipkin et al. (2022). Each reference map was projected into the participant's target geometry and correlated spatially with every network map. For each reference, the 3 most correlated networks received its label, in rank order, so a network could receive several labels. Networks that no reference selected kept numeric identifiers. These labels describe spatial similarity and are not functional localizer results.\par}
